= Bit (simple) =

A '''bit''' helps measure information. In computers, a bit can also mean one binary digit: 0 or 1. These meanings are connected, but they are not interchangeable.

== A coin example ==
Imagine tossing a fair coin. Before you see it, heads and tails are equally likely. Its uncertainty is '''one bit'''. If a coin always lands heads, you could still record its result using a 0 or 1, but you learn nothing new from the toss: its uncertainty is '''zero bits'''. With a coin that lands heads nine times in ten, the average uncertainty is about '''0.469 bits'''.

A bit is therefore not just a physical switch, nor does every 0-or-1 symbol automatically mean one bit of new information.

== Beyond computers ==
Bits are used to study [[Information theory/Simple|information]], the efficiency of communication, the uncertainty of biological signals, and how many messages a system can distinguish. Computers use binary representations because engineered devices can reliably distinguish between two states. The same mathematics can describe phenomena outside computers.

A [[Qubit|qubit]] is a different quantum-information concept. A bit of information also does not tell you whether a statement is true, useful, or meaningful.

